<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Siena</h1><p class="st-meta">1125 CE – 1555 CE (approx.) · capital Siena</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Florence's great Tuscan rival, a banking and wool republic whose Palazzo Pubblico frescoes picture the ideals of good government.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Siena's citizens took power from their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in the early 12th century, and consuls are recorded by 1125. Lying on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-francigena">Via Francigena</a> pilgrim road, the commune prospered from banking and trade and expanded its territory across southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Tuscany</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Siena was generally Ghibelline and crushed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-florence">Florence</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montaperti">Montaperti</a> in 1260 under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/provenzan-salvani">Provenzan Salvani</a>. From 1287 to 1355 the merchant government of the Nine built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palazzo-pubblico-siena">Palazzo Pubblico</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrogio-lorenzetti">Ambrogio Lorenzetti</a> painted his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allegory-of-good-and-bad-government">Allegory of Good and Bad Government</a> in 1338–1339. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duccio">Duccio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simone-martini">Simone Martini</a> made the city a centre of painting. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1348 halted its growth; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banca-monte-dei-paschi-di-siena">Monte dei Paschi</a> bank dates from 1472.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After a period of rule by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandolfo-petrucci">Pandolfo Petrucci</a>, Siena rebelled against a Spanish garrison in 1552. Imperial and Florentine troops besieged it until it surrendered in April 1555. Exiles continued as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-siena-at-montalcino">Republic of Siena at Montalcino</a>, and Siena was granted to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-florence">Duchy of Florence</a> in 1557.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-siena.jpg" alt="Republic of Siena" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Own work · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Siena.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1125 CE – 1555 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Siena</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Provenzan Salvani, Pandolfo Petrucci</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tuscan Italian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Siena, Commune of Siena</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1340&amp;focus=republic-of-siena" aria-label="Republic of Siena on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4953 641 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Siena lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5273 897L5229 694M5273 897L4910 989M5273 897L5270 883M5273 897L5277 905"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M4910 989h0M5270 883h0M5277 905h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5273 897h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5273 897h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Siena. <a href="/atlas?year=1340&amp;focus=republic-of-siena">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
